OE ca.1205 gedwimer/gedwomer "sorcery"

Gygax did quite a bit to improve the arcane vocabulary of a generation
or three of RPG geeks, but he did little actual invention (at least in
terms of language, and arguably in general).

That Teitelman played D&D is at least possible (if I have my dates
right), but D&D wasn't really a mainstream phenomenon the way it is
now. (Xerox PARC got its start in the early 1970s, about the same time
original D&D evolved from the "Chainmail" mass-miniatures system.)

However, you're right in suggesting that in-game punning on dwimmer/
dweomer would be a nethackish thing to do.


